1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
When you build an MCP server, the real question isn't what primitives are, it's how you stand one up.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:14,000
You register your tools, resources, and prompts, then route the requests clients send and format the responses.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,000
This lesson walks through registering each primitive and the mechanics behind a working server.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Everything a server does starts with registering its primitives, so let's take the three in turn.

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:33,000
First, tools. You register each tool with a name, a description, and an input schema, and the client

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,000
invokes it by sending tools slash call.

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Second, resources. Each resource gets a URI and is served read-only through resources slash read, so

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,000
the host can pull it in as context whenever it needs to.

9
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Third, prompts. You register a prompt with a name and optional arguments, and when the client sends

10
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,000
prompts slash get, the server returns it as messages.

11
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Tools you register to be called. Resources to be read. Prompts to be fetched. Same registration

12
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:05,000
idea, three different retrieval methods.

13
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Get these registered correctly, and the rest is routing.

14
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Let's zoom in on registering a tool, since it's the primitive that does work.

15
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:21,000
At minimum, a tool definition needs a name, a description, and an input schema that spells out its parameters.

16
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:29,000
You can add optional fields too, a display title, an output schema for structured results, and annotations.

17
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:35,000
On the wire, clients find your tools by calling tools slash list, which returns everything you've

18
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,000
registered, and then they run one with tools slash call.

19
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:45,000
One piece of naming advice, prefer a namespaced name like calculator arithmetic over a bare

20
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
calculate, so tools from different servers don't collide.

21
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Register the definition, advertise it through list, execute it through call. That's the full life of

22
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:57,000
a tool on your server.

23
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:06,000
There's a clean split running through the whole protocol, discovery versus execution, and it's worth seeing directly.

24
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:13,000
On the discovery side, every primitive gives clients a list method. Tools slash list, resources

25
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:20,000
slash list, and prompts slash list each let a client find out what your server actually exposes

26
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:21,000
before it uses anything.

27
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:26,000
On the execution side, the calls are separate and specific.

28
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:34,000
Tools slash call runs a tool and returns its result. Resources slash read hands back the data for a

29
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,000
URI and prompts slash get returns a filled in template.

30
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:45,000
So a client first asks what's here, then asks for the specific thing it wants. Discovery is always a

31
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:48,000
list. Retrieval and action each have their own method.

32
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Keeping those two phases straight makes the request flow easy to reason about.

33
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Now resources, which you register a little differently because they're data, not actions. Each

34
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:04,000
resource gets a unique URI, and that URI is how a client addresses it.

35
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:11,000
You serve resources through two methods, resources slash list to enumerate what's available, and

36
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
resources slash read to return the actual contents.

37
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,000
You can attach optional fields like a name, a description, and a mime type so the client knows what it's getting.

38
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:28,000
One handy feature, templated URIs. Instead of registering a thousand files one by one, you register

39
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:32,000
a URI pattern that stands in for a whole family of resources.

40
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Remember, all of this is read only. Registering a resource exposes data for the application to

41
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:42,000
inject. It never gives the model something to execute.

42
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Underneath the primitives, a server has four jobs to handle. So let's name them.

43
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:55,000
First, capability declaration. When the server starts up, it announces which primitives it supports

44
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,000
so a connecting client knows what to expect.

45
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Second, request routing. As list, read, and call methods come in, the server dispatches each one to

46
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:07,000
the right handler.

47
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:14,000
Third, response formatting. Each handler returns its result in the shape the protocol defines, as

48
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:16,000
content the client can actually use.

49
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Fourth, error signaling, and this one has a wrinkle. Protocol errors, like an unknown tool, are

50
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:27,000
reported one way, and tool execution errors are reported another.

51
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:34,000
Capability declaration, request routing, response formatting, error signaling. Four concerns, and a

52
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,000
real server handles all of them.

53
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:42,000
Prompts round out the set, and remember, these belong to the user. You register a prompt with a name

54
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:47,000
and an optional list of arguments, each argument describing what the user can fill in.

55
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Clients discover prompts with a list call, then fetch a specific one by sending prompts slash get,

56
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:58,000
and your server responds with a set of messages that seed the conversation.

57
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:04,000
Because the person is the one choosing, prompts usually surface in the host as slash commands or

58
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
menu items. That's the contrast to keep in mind.

59
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:14,000
A tool is something the model decides to call, but a prompt just sits there as an option until a

60
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:18,000
user deliberately picks it. You register it, the user reaches for it.

61
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:27,000
The error field follows the same spelling rule as input schema. MCP flags a failed execution with

62
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:34,000
isError in camel case on the JSON RPC wire, and the Cloud Messages API signals the same failure with

63
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:35,000
isError in snake case.

64
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:42,000
Note what isError covers. It marks a tool that ran and failed with the details in the result content

65
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,000
so the model can read them and adjust.

66
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:52,000
A protocol-level failure, like a call to a tool you never registered, comes back as a JSON RPC error

67
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:53,000
instead, not as a result.

68
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:59,000
Keep the two channels separate in your handlers. When you're writing server code against the MCP

69
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:05,000
spec, use the camel case names. When you're handling the API response in your application, use the

70
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:09,000
snake case ones. Get the layer right and you'll get the spelling right.

71
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:14,000
One more piece of authoring to know, and it's about scope.

72
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:20,000
Roots are how a client tells a server which file system paths or URIs are in scope for the work.

73
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:26,000
Here's the important part. Roots are advisory. A well-behaved server should respect them, but

74
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,000
nothing in the protocol enforces it.

75
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:36,000
A local STDIO server runs with the full file system access of its operating system user, whether or

76
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:43,000
not roots are declared. So, treat roots as a scoping convention, and get real isolation from the

77
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:47,000
environment, OS-level sandboxing, containers, and file permissions.

78
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:55,000
When you build a server, honor the roots the client declares. When you run one, sandbox it as though it might not.

79
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:01,000
Here's the bottom line. Authoring a server is registering the three primitives and wiring the

80
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:06,000
mechanics around them. You declare capabilities, route requests, and format responses.

81
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:13,000
Discovering with list and then calling, reading, or getting. That covers authoring and registering

82
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:15,000
an MCP server. I'll see you in the next one.

